/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Most of these devices put one winding on the stator and one on the rotor that follows the shaft.
   - By Faraday's law the output is an AC voltage at the excitation frequency f, with amplitude proportional to M and therefore to cos θ.
   - The output is a carrier: a fixed-frequency AC wave whose envelope follows the shaft.
   - An induction potentiometer is a rotary transformer with a distributed winding.
   - eo is the output rms voltage (V); k is the coupling constant, less than 1; Ex is the rms excitation voltage (V); θ is the shaft angle from electrical zero.
   - Here θ is measured from electrical zero, the position of minimum coupling, so the cos becomes a sin.
   - At the null the output is not exactly zero.
   - A synchro transmitter has a single-phase rotor on the shaft and a three-phase stator: three identical windings spaced 120° electrical.
   - The control transformer looks the same but works in reverse.
   - Near the null sin Δθ ≈ Δθ in radians, so the output measures the error directly, and its phase against the excitation says which way the error lies.
*/
