/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Drive the probe coil with an alternating current.
   - The coil impedance is Z = R + jωL.
   - Z is coil impedance in Ω; R is coil resistance in Ω; L is coil inductance in H; j is the imaginary unit; ω = 2πf is angular frequency in rad/s; f is excitation frequency in Hz.
   - High excitation frequency helps.
   - δ is skin depth in m; ρ is target resistivity in Ω·m; ω = 2πf in rad/s; μ = μ₀μr is target permeability in H/m.
   - A typical probe has a small coil wound on a ferrite or ceramic former.
   - The bridge compares the probe-coil impedance with a reference arm.
   - Some instruments use a phase-sensitive demodulator.
   - The sensing range is short, of the order of the coil diameter; a 5 mm coil gives a few millimetres of useful range.
   - The linear range is small, so do not extrapolate the calibration from zero to large gaps.
*/
