/* book Worked example 2: Q: Find the skin depth in aluminium (ρ = 2.65 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m, μr = 1) and in stainless steel (ρ = 7.2 × 10⁻⁷ Ω·m, μr ≈ 1) at 100 kHz and 1 MHz. Can a 0.5 mm aluminium sheet be measured for lift-off at 100 kHz without its thickness affecting the reading?
   1. δ = √(2ρ/(ωμ)) with μ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H/m and ω = 2πf.
   2. Aluminium, 100 kHz: ωμ = 2π × 10⁵ × 4π × 10⁻⁷ = 0.7896; δ = √(2 × 2.65 × 10⁻⁸/0.7896) = √(6.71 × 10⁻⁸) = 2.59 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.259 mm.
   3. Aluminium, 1 MHz: δ scales as 1/√f, so δ = 0.259/√10 = 0.082 mm.
   4. Stainless steel, 100 kHz: δ = √(2 × 7.2 × 10⁻⁷/0.7896) = 1.35 mm; at 1 MHz 0.43 mm. The higher resistivity lets the field go five times deeper.
   5. Rule: the target should be at least 3δ thick. At 100 kHz 3δ = 0.78 mm, more than the 0.5 mm sheet, so thickness would change the reading. At 1 MHz 3δ = 0.25 mm, so the sheet looks infinitely thick.
   Answer: Aluminium 0.259 mm at 100 kHz and 0.082 mm at 1 MHz; stainless 1.35 mm and 0.43 mm. Use 1 MHz for the 0.5 mm aluminium sheet. */
