/* book exam traps: Using one calibration for all metals: aluminium, brass and steel have different conductivity and permeability, so the same gap gives different volts. | Dropping the sign of sensitivity: −2.5 V/mm means output falls as gap rises; a rising output means the gap is smaller. | Treating the range as zero to infinity: the linear range is only a small band around the set gap, roughly set by coil diameter. | Confusing lift-off with target thickness: lift-off is the probe-to-metal gap, and a nonconductive coating on the metal simply adds to it; the metal’s own thickness is a different quantity, and it matters only when the metal is thinner than about three skin dep | Calling it an ON/OFF proximity switch: this transducer gives an analog measurement; the switching version is a different use, see 3.5. */
