/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - One sensor cannot switch at the same distance for every target, so the datasheet fixes the target, temperature and supply voltage first.
   - Rated sensing distance Sn is the switching distance with a standard square mild-steel target at 20–25 °C and rated supply voltage.
   - S is usable switching distance in mm for the actual target; K is the material correction factor from the datasheet, 1.00 for mild steel; Sn is rated sensing distance in mm.
   - Hysteresis is the gap between ON distance as the target approaches and OFF distance as it leaves; release is farther.
   - Flush (shielded) sensors have a metal ring round the coil, so the field leaves only from the front face; they can sit in a steel bracket.
   - The sensing element is a coil on a ferrite core behind the face, driven by an oscillator at roughly 100 kHz to 1 MHz.
   - Iron and steel are magnetic and conductive, so they change inductance too and give the longest range.
   - The output is a transistor switch, not a relay contact.
   - A capacitive sensor uses a flat metal electrode behind the face as one capacitor plate.
   - C is capacitance in farads; ε₀ = 8.85 × 10−12 F/m; εr is relative permittivity of the gap material (about 1 for air, 80 for water); A is effective plate area in m²; d is gap in m.
*/
