/* book Worked example: Q: An inductive sensor has Sn = 10 mm for mild steel. Datasheet K = 0.50 for brass and 0.70 for stainless steel. (a) A brass target is 5.2 mm away. Is switching guaranteed? (b) What is the maximum brass distance? (c) A stainless-steel target is 7.3 mm away. Is switching guaranteed?
   1. Sn = 10 mm applies to mild steel, K = 1.00. Other metals scale by K.
   2. Brass: Sbrass = K × Sn = 0.50 × 10 mm = 5.0 mm.
   3. Brass at 5.2 mm is beyond 5.0 mm. Operate point can reach 1.1 × 5.0 = 5.5 mm, so detection is not guaranteed; reliable only inside 0.9 × 5.0 = 4.5 mm.
   4. Maximum brass distance is 5.0 mm.
   5. Stainless: Sss = 0.70 × 10 mm = 7.0 mm.
   6. Stainless at 7.3 mm is beyond 7.0 mm. Operate point can reach 1.1 × 7.0 = 7.7 mm, so not guaranteed; reliable only inside 0.9 × 7.0 = 6.3 mm.
   7. Both targets are in the uncertain band beyond corrected distance: may switch, but not reliably.
   Answer: Brass at 5.2 mm against Sbrass = 5.0 mm: not guaranteed; reliable only inside 4.5 mm. Stainless steel at 7.3 mm against Sss = 7.0 mm: not guaranteed; reliable only inside 6.3 mm. */
