/* book exam traps: Using Sn for every metal. Sn is for standard mild steel; other materials need S = K × Sn, with K below 1 for brass, aluminium and copper. | Ignoring hysteresis. Release distance is larger than operate point, so a target that just switched ON may not switch OFF when it moves back by the same small amount. | Confusing flush and non-flush. A shielded sensor can sit flush in steel without losing range because its ring keeps the field at the face. An unshielded sensor reaches farther but needs free space around the face; metal beside it changes the field. | Swapping NPN and PNP. A sinking input expects a sourcing (PNP) sensor; the wrong pair gives no signal or a short through the output transistor. | Calling capacitive type a distance meter. It is a switch, and range depends strongly on εr, so water switches from far away while thin plastic must come closer. */
